    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p><strong>Frozen shoulder is scarring and tightening of the shoulder's capsule, not a cartilage, tendon or calcium problem.</strong> It usually runs one to three years and then eases; in a follow-up of 83 patients tracked 2 to 27 years, 94% regained normal motion with no treatment at all.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The cause is too much collagen, not too little:</strong> the capsule lays down dense type I and type III collagen and contracts.</li>
        <li><strong>Diabetes is the single biggest risk factor:</strong> reported in roughly 10% to 20% of people with diabetes, against 2% to 5% of everyone else.</li>
        <li><strong>Steroid injection plus gentle movement helps pain fastest,</strong> but by 12 months the big trials show little difference between approaches.</li>
        <li><strong>No supplement has been tested in frozen shoulder</strong> — collagen included, and that applies to Skelecoll as much as to any other brand.</li>
      </ul>
