      <h2 id="what">What is frozen shoulder, and why won't it move?</h2>
      <p>Your shoulder will not move because the bag of tissue around the joint has scarred and shrunk. That bag is the glenohumeral capsule. In frozen shoulder — doctors call it adhesive capsulitis, and many Indian clinics still write "periarthritis shoulder" — the capsule becomes inflamed, then fibrotic, then physically shorter. The ball and socket are usually fine. The container around them has tightened.</p>
      <p>This is the detail that makes most supplement advice for frozen shoulder nonsensical. A 2025 review of current concepts in <em>Current Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation Reports</em> describes ordinary capsular fibroblasts switching into myofibroblasts and depositing new, disorganised collagen, concentrated at the rotator interval and the coracohumeral ligament. A 2017 review in <em>European Journal of Inflammation</em> points to TGF-beta driving dense type I and type III collagen accumulation in the capsule.</p>
      <p>So frozen shoulder is a disease of excess collagen in the wrong place, laid down too thickly and pulled too tight. It is not a shortage of collagen, and it is not worn cartilage — that is <a href="/pages/knee-osteoarthritis-grades">osteoarthritis</a>, a different problem with different evidence. Anyone selling you collagen to loosen a stiff capsule has the mechanism backwards.</p>
      <p>The one thing that distinguishes it on examination is loss of external rotation: turning the forearm outwards with the elbow at your side. Rotator cuff problems hurt when you lift. Frozen shoulder stops you turning, and it stops you whether you try or someone else tries for you.</p>

