      <h2 id="how-long">How long does frozen shoulder last?</h2>
      <p>Expect one to three years, in three overlapping phases. That is a brutal answer and it is the honest one.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Freezing (roughly 6 weeks to 9 months).</strong> Pain leads. It is worst at night, and it is often worse than the stiffness at this stage.</li>
        <li><strong>Frozen (roughly 4 to 12 months).</strong> Pain settles, stiffness takes over. Most people can sleep again but cannot reach a back pocket or fasten a blouse.</li>
        <li><strong>Thawing (several months to a year or more).</strong> Movement returns gradually, usually without anything dramatic marking the turn.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Published stage timings vary considerably between sources, so treat those ranges as a shape rather than a schedule. What is better established is the destination. Vastamäki and colleagues followed 83 patients (84 shoulders, 56 of them women) for a mean of 9 years and up to 27, and reported that range of motion returned to match the opposite shoulder in 94% of the untreated group and 91% of those treated non-operatively. Their conclusion was that spontaneous frozen shoulder recovers to normal function and motion without treatment in the large majority of people.</p>
      <p>That is not the same as saying everyone ends up perfect. A separate study of conservatively treated patients, published in <em>Orthopaedics &amp; Traumatology: Surgery &amp; Research</em> in 2019, found 39.7% still had a measurable movement deficit at a mean of 41.8 months. Both findings can be true: nearly everyone gets their life back, and a sizeable minority never quite gets the last few degrees back.</p>

      <h2 id="diabetes">Why is frozen shoulder so common in Indian diabetics?</h2>
      <p>Because high blood glucose appears to change how collagen behaves, and India has a very large number of people living with diabetes. This is the most useful section of this article for most Indian readers, and it has nothing to do with buying anything.</p>
      <p>A 2024 narrative review in the <em>Journal of Clinical Medicine</em> gathered the figures: frozen shoulder is reported in roughly 10% to 20% of people with diabetes against 2% to 5% of the general population, and two Indian studies cited in it reported rates of 37.5% and 27.4%. A meta-analysis of six case-control studies found people with diabetes more than three times as likely to develop it. Running the association the other way, pooled data suggest around 30% of people presenting with frozen shoulder have diabetes.</p>
      <p>The leading explanation is glycation — glucose binding to collagen and cross-linking it, so the capsule repairs abnormally and stiffens more readily. It is a plausible mechanism rather than a proven one, and you should treat anyone who states it as settled with suspicion.</p>
      <p>Two practical consequences. First, if you have developed a frozen shoulder and have never had your blood sugar checked, get an HbA1c. Several of the studies above explicitly recommend screening, and in an Indian OPD a stiff shoulder is a genuinely common way for undiagnosed type 2 diabetes to first show itself. Second, both shoulders are more often involved when diabetes is present, so a second frozen shoulder a year or two later is not bad luck.</p>
      <p>Whether diabetes makes the outcome worse is less clear than the internet suggests. Studies disagree: some report poorer forward flexion, internal rotation and post-operative shoulder scores in diabetic patients, while a follow-up averaging nearly ten years found no significant difference in overall shoulder score, and one study found the condition actually lasted longer in the group <em>without</em> diabetes. Mixed evidence, stated as mixed.</p>
      <p>Thyroid disease is also listed as a risk factor across these reviews, though with weaker numbers behind it than diabetes. If that applies to you, our note on <a href="/pages/collagen-and-thyroid-medication">collagen and thyroid medication</a> covers the timing question separately.</p>

      <h2 id="treatment">What actually treats frozen shoulder?</h2>
      <p>Treatment buys you comfort and speed, not a different ending. The largest trial ever run in frozen shoulder found that three quite different interventions landed in the same place after a year.</p>
      <p>UK FROST, published in <em>The Lancet</em> in 2020, screened 914 people and randomised 503 to early structured physiotherapy, manipulation under anaesthesia, or arthroscopic capsular release. At 12 months the mean Oxford Shoulder Scores were 37.2, 38.3 and 40.3 respectively, and the authors concluded none of the three was clinically superior. Manipulation had the highest probability of being cost-effective. Capsular release carried eight serious adverse events against two for manipulation.</p>
      <p>For the earlier, more painful stage, injections have the better short-term record. A systematic review of randomised trials comparing corticosteroid injection against physiotherapy found a significant advantage for injection on shoulder disability at 6 to 7 weeks that persisted at 12 to 16 weeks, and by 52 weeks the difference had largely gone. A 2025 randomised trial in <em>Scientific Reports</em> comparing intra-articular corticosteroid against multimodal physiotherapy, both alongside home exercise, found both effective in the short term for pain, disability and range of motion.</p>

      <p>The Cochrane review on this question — Page and colleagues, <em>Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews</em>, 2014 — looked specifically at manual therapy and exercise for adhesive capsulitis, including a comparison of six weeks of manual therapy plus exercise against a glucocorticoid injection in general practice. We could not reach the full text from here to quote its pooled numbers, so we are not going to put a figure on it. The direction of the literature is consistent, though: hands-on treatment and exercise are worth having, and they do not clearly outperform an injection.</p>

