      <p>One finding deserves more attention than it gets, because it contradicts what many patients are told. Diercks and Stevens followed 77 patients for two years in the <em>Journal of Shoulder and Elbow Surgery</em> in 2004, comparing intensive physiotherapy — stretching and mobilisation pushed past the pain threshold — against "supervised neglect", meaning pain-guided movement within comfortable limits. The gentler group did better: 89% reached a Constant score above 80, against 63% of the intensive group. The authors concluded that aggressive stretching beyond pain may be harmful, particularly early on.</p>
      <p>It is a single study from 2004, with 77 patients, and it should not be treated as the final word. But if your shoulder is being forcibly pulled by anyone — a physiotherapist, a well-meaning relative, someone doing vigorous maalish — that is the study to know about. Move it daily, move it often, stop at pain. The same pain-guided principle runs through our <a href="/pages/exercises-for-knee-pain">home exercise guide for knee pain</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="supplements">Does collagen or any supplement help frozen shoulder?</h2>
      <p>No supplement has been shown to treat frozen shoulder, and that includes ours. We searched for a randomised trial of oral collagen peptides in adhesive capsulitis and could not find one. The nearest registered trial of a collagen, hyaluronic acid, vitamin C and manganese combination is in rotator cuff tendinopathy, and it specifically <em>excludes</em> people with adhesive capsulitis.</p>
      <p>This matters because the pages ranking for "supplements for frozen shoulder" say otherwise. Several assert that collagen, "especially type II", improves mobility and reduces discomfort in frozen shoulder. None of them cites a study in frozen shoulder, because there isn't one. Others borrow dose figures from osteoarthritis reviews and present them as frozen shoulder evidence. Our own page on <a href="/pages/types-of-collagen">collagen types</a> explains why the type II framing is mostly marketing in the first place.</p>

