      <p>Two nutrients come up often enough to address directly. On <strong>vitamin C</strong>, the only study worth mentioning is a rat model of shoulder contracture where prophylactic vitamin C appeared to reduce thickening of the axillary recess. That is an animal study, it tested prevention rather than treatment, and it does not show benefit in people. On <strong>vitamin D</strong>, a 2021 retrospective review in <em>Annals of Medical Research</em> found low serum levels may track with shoulder pain in adhesive capsulitis but had no effect on functional outcomes. Low vitamin D is worth correcting on its own merits — our note on <a href="/pages/vitamin-d-deficiency-symptoms">vitamin D deficiency</a> covers why, in a country where deficiency is common despite the sunshine — but correcting it is not a frozen shoulder treatment.</p>
      <p>Where a product like Skelecoll genuinely fits is the background, not the diagnosis. If you have a frozen shoulder you are also likely to be in your forties, fifties or sixties, moving less than usual for a year or more, and possibly diabetic. Protein intake, vitamin D status and general joint and bone support all matter in that situation, and they matter whether or not your shoulder is stiff. That is a reasonable thing to pay attention to. It is not a treatment for adhesive capsulitis, and we are not going to describe it as one.</p>

