      <h2 id="doctor">When to see a doctor rather than wait</h2>
      <p>Frozen shoulder is a clinical diagnosis, and the main reason to be examined is that several more serious problems can imitate it. Do not self-diagnose a shoulder that will not move. See a doctor promptly if any of the following apply:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>The stiffness followed a fall, a dislocation or any significant injury.</li>
        <li>You have fever, redness or heat over the joint, or you feel unwell — joint infection needs same-day assessment.</li>
        <li>There is pain radiating into the chest, jaw or left arm, or breathlessness with it. Shoulder pain can be cardiac. Treat it as an emergency.</li>
        <li>You have numbness, tingling or weakness in the hand or arm, which points to the neck rather than the shoulder.</li>
        <li>There is unexplained weight loss, night sweats, a history of cancer, or a lump.</li>
        <li>You cannot lift the arm at all after an injury, suggesting a cuff tear rather than a capsule problem.</li>
        <li>Your blood sugar has never been tested — not urgent, but get the HbA1c done while you are there.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>If the diagnosis is confirmed, ask specifically about an injection during the painful phase and about physiotherapy you can actually access. Those two conversations change your next six months more than any purchase will.</p>

