      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>How long does frozen shoulder take to heal?</summary>
          <p>Usually one to three years, through a painful freezing phase, a stiff frozen phase and a gradual thawing phase. In a follow-up of 83 patients tracked 2 to 27 years, 94% of the untreated group recovered motion matching their other shoulder. A separate 2019 study found 39.7% still had some measurable movement deficit at a mean of 41.8 months, so a minority do not get every degree back.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does collagen help frozen shoulder?</summary>
          <p>There is no randomised trial of collagen peptides in frozen shoulder, so there is no evidence either way. The mechanism also points the wrong direction: frozen shoulder involves the capsule depositing too much type I and type III collagen and contracting, not a shortage of collagen. Pages claiming type II collagen improves frozen shoulder mobility do not cite a study in the condition.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Should I push through the pain when exercising a frozen shoulder?</summary>
          <p>No. Diercks and Stevens compared intensive stretching past the pain threshold against pain-guided movement in 77 patients over two years, and the gentler group did better: 89% reached a good shoulder score against 63%. Move the shoulder daily and often, but stop at pain, especially in the early painful phase.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is frozen shoulder a sign of diabetes?</summary>
          <p>It can be the first sign. Frozen shoulder is reported in roughly 10% to 20% of people with diabetes against 2% to 5% of the general population, and pooled data suggest about 30% of people presenting with it have diabetes. If you develop a frozen shoulder and have not had your blood sugar checked, ask for an HbA1c.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Which treatment works best for frozen shoulder?</summary>
          <p>Nothing has been shown to be clearly best. UK FROST randomised 503 patients to physiotherapy, manipulation under anaesthesia or arthroscopic capsular release and found none superior at 12 months. For the early painful stage, corticosteroid injection beats physiotherapy alone on disability at 6 to 16 weeks, but that advantage has largely gone by a year.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can frozen shoulder affect both shoulders?</summary>
          <p>Yes, and it is more likely if you have diabetes. Bilateral involvement is reported in roughly 6% to 17% of patients overall and is described as more common in diabetic groups. The second shoulder usually starts months or years after the first rather than at the same time.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Will vitamin D fix my frozen shoulder?</summary>
          <p>No. A 2021 retrospective review in Annals of Medical Research found low vitamin D may be associated with shoulder pain in adhesive capsulitis but had no effect on functional results. Correcting a deficiency is worth doing for bone and muscle health regardless, but it is not a treatment for the stiffness.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does frozen shoulder come back after it resolves?</summary>
          <p>Recurrence in the same shoulder is uncommon, and long-term data on it are sparse — authors of long-term follow-up studies note there is little literature beyond ten years. The more common pattern is the other shoulder becoming affected later, particularly in people with diabetes.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

